  "enhanced_information": "The presence of yellowish lesions with yellowish liquid in a patient with hyperuricemia and inflammatory joint pain is suggestive of tophaceous gout. Synovial fluid analysis is crucial for diagnosis, and a cell count between 2000 and 60,000 / μl indicates septic arthritis requiring specific treatment. Uric acid levels alone may not always be well above the reference range in tophaceous gout. The diagnostic technique of choice is not arthroscopy but rather a complete study of the fluid."
